\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Brazil's Cardoso says IMF talks advancing. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Schomberg \par \uc2 836 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 1999\par 00:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRASILIA, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Brazilian President Fernando Cardoso met with International Monetary Fund negotiators on Wednesday and said crucial talks on how to stem a deep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Latin America's giant economy were making progress. \par \par \uc2  "The situation in Brazil is developing the way we wanted ... with the return of confidence in the markets," Cardoso told reporters as the negotiations resumed. "We took strong necessary decisions. We are advancing in talks with (the IMF)." \par \par \uc2  Cardoso also said he would not allow the days of roaring inflation to return, a major concern after a near 35 percent devaluation of the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last three weeks. \par \par \uc2  The real ended a day of light trading at 1.79 per dollar, down 4 cents from Tuesday's closing price but 32 percent weaker since Brazil's worst crisis in years erupted on Jan. 13. \par \par \uc2  Traders said markets were still happy with Tuesday's surprise nomination of Arminio Fraga, an aide to billionaire market mogul George Soros, as the new president of Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The government hopes Fraga will bring a market-savvy edge to its attempts to regain credibility among foreign investors. Left-wing leader Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva said the appointment was "submission to the interests of international bankers." \par \par \uc2  But all eyes were on the three-day-old talks which are key to Brazil's hopes of averting a Russia-style crash. \par \par \uc2  Brazil and the IMF are hammering out a new set of fiscal and monetary targets to help narrow Brazil's huge budget deficit and pave the way for a second $9 billion instalment of loans from a $41.5 billion rescue package assembled by the IMF in November. \par \par \uc2  Original targets are now impossible to meet after the devaluation and a subsequent hike in interest rates to nearly 40 percent a year added to the already huge cost of Brazil's 340-billion-reais debt mountain. \par \par \uc2  The talks were also likely to focus on new ways for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its newly floating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. Cardoso ruled out the possibility of introducing a "virtual band" to peg the real against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday, the real plunged to a low of 2.18 to the dollar as fears of drastic action by the government prompted some Brazilians to run to the banks to pull out their savings. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's number two, First Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer, decided to stay in Brasilia an extra day until Thursday, but the talks were expected to continue after his departure. \par \par \uc2  Another top IMF official said on Wednesday that Brazil and the fund were on the same track and that, despite criticism, the country's high interest rate policy was the right one. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly the tightness in monetary policy will continue," said IMF Deputy Managing Director Alassane Ouattara. "Our experience in Asia has shown that it is important to see a firm monetary framework at the beginning of a crisis." \par \par \uc2  Some leading economists fear Brazil's interest rates, designed to keep a grip on inflation, will plunge the country into a major recession and add to the risk of a devastating debt default that could set economic development back by years. \par \par \uc2  With Brazil facing at best a fierce recession, officials and businessmen in other South American countries are increasingly concerned for their own economies. \par \par \uc2  A senior Argentine industry representative claimed on Wednesday that Brazilian exports to its southern neighbour doubled after the devaluation of the real. \par \par \uc2  So far, however, the Argentine government has resisted calls from business leaders to put up barriers to Brazilian exports. \par \par \uc2  Cardoso was due to discuss the crisis with his Argentine counterpart Carlos Menem on Feb. 12, and a summit with the presidents of the other countries in the Mercosur customs union - Paraguay and Uruguay - was under preparation, officials said. \par \par \uc2  "We are going to come out of all this turbulence with an even stronger feeling that we must reinforce the relationship within Mercosur," Cardoso said after meeting with Uruguayan president Julio Maria Sanguinetti on Wednesday in Brasilia. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's priority for now, however, is smothering any return of sky-high inflation. Only five years ago, prices were rising by 5,000 percent a year, requiring Brazilians to carry bundles of grubby bank notes just to pay for bus fares and newspapers. \par \par \uc2  After nearly zero inflation last year, prices could rise by as much as 80 percent in 1999 on the back of the devaluation, according to some private predictions. \par \par \uc2  Should they prove correct, the government will be virtually helpless to prevent the return of indexation, the practice of raising pay and prices to compensate for expected inflation. That would plunge Brazil back into economic chaos. \par \par \uc2  "The big worry of the government is to avoid the return of inflation," said a presidential spokesman after Cardoso urged food industry executives not to follow the lead of local carmakers and other manufacturers who have begun raising prices. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv23059yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
