\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil-IMF talks focus after bank drama. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jeremy Smith \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 1999\par 06:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - World market attention should return Wednesday to protracted talks aiming to save Brazil from an Asian-style meltdown, the day after the nation stunned markets by suddenly naming a new \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief. \par \par \uc2  A team from the International Monetary Fund (IMF) has been huddled with government officials since Sunday over how to rebuild Brazil's credibility after its surprise devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, three weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  The two sides hope to hammer out new targets for Brazil's budget, interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules following the devaluation, which was outside the terms of the deal struck by the government with the IMF last November. \par \par \uc2  Brazil stunned markets again on Tuesday when it entrusted its huge crisis-torn economy to an aide of billionaire speculator George Soros, replacing its interim president Francisco Lopes. \par \par \uc2  Arminio Fraga, 42, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  director who was most recently managing director of Soros's powerful investment fund in New York, is expected to bring priceless experience of working in volatile markets to the Brazilian government. \par \par \uc2  But as the dust settled after the surprise announcement of a second \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president in three weeks, economists said while the replacement would give initial confidence to Brazil's economy, it could not mask its serious underlying problems. \par \par \uc2  "The real challenge now for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the government as a whole, is to stop the devaluation from causing inflation to rise again," said Mauro Schneider, chief economist at ING Bank in Sao Paulo. "There's no escape, the main thrust has to be fiscal." \par \par \uc2  "I don't think replacing Lopes with Fraga at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resolves the problems of the external financing and investor confidence. The same set of problems are still there," said economist Siobhan Manning at Paine Webber in New York. \par \par \uc2  One of Fraga's first tasks may be to hammer out an agreement with the IMF for it to speed up the release of a second $9 billion tranche of international loans to see Brazil through its crisis. \par \par \uc2  Brazil agreed last November to a series of fiscal and monetary targets in return for a $41.5 billion IMF-sponsored international rescue deal to prevent Latin America's giant from spiraling into an economic black hole. \par \par \uc2  In the meantime, the country is engaged in a desperate juggling act to keep a grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cut its massive budget deficit and put a lid on inflation. \par \par \uc2  However, the higher cost of Brazil's huge debt mountain resulting from the devaluation has dashed any chance the government had of meeting the targets, now being revised. \par \par \uc2  The talks in Brasilia should also focus on new ways for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to protect the real against the kind of wild fluctuations suffered last week, when it sank below two-per-dollar for the first time. \par \par \uc2  The real, introduced in 1994 as Brazil's inflation-busting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and once stronger than the dollar, has lost some 35 percent of its value since the government eased its rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy last month and allowed a free-float. \par \par \uc2  IMF First Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer arrived in Brazil late on Monday to speed up the negotiations. \par \par \uc2  Economists said while the IMF team might be wary about Brazil's sudden move to replace its \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief in the midst of the crisis, it was likely to view it as a short-term confidence boost for the world's eighth-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  "Ultimately the IMF will be a little wary about the sudden replacement of governors because it shows that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not independent, but that's more of a medium-term concern," Paine Webber's Manning said. \par \par \uc2  "At this point anything to restore confidence is going to be well regarded and received by markets and the IMF...though it's probably a little early to expect any revision of targets. They only just got there and there's a lot to hash over." \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Brazil's economy already looks well on its way to a recession that could mean an economic contraction of five percent this year. \par \par \uc2  Brazil could then be left with no choice but to default on its rapidly expanding debt - a devastating worst-case scenario that would wreck the country's financial system and ruin its already rock-bottom ratings among investors. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv23059h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
