\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil cenbank chief sees IMF targets maintained. \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 1999\par 19:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRASILIA, Jan 29 (Reuters) - Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Francisco Lopes said Friday he saw no need for the country to revise targets for its primary budget surplus and net public sector debt agreed with the International Monetary Fund last year. \par \par \uc2  Brazil is going through its deepest financial crisis in years after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  relaxed its stiff \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on Jan. 13. Since then, the real has lost about 40 percent of its value against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Despite the devaluation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees net public sector debt in 1999 remaining below 46.7 percent of gross domestic product, the level agreed with the IMF, Lopes said in an interview with foreign correspondents. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets despite the real's plunge through a psychological barrier of two reais per dollar on Friday. \par \par \uc2  He said foreign reserves remained at $36 billion Friday and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would begin daily reports on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve levels to show when the bank had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At the moment, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders judge by the levels of dollars flowing out of the country whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has started selling dollars to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are going to start reports on Monday, given this situation, this absurd thing that people are reading the outflow statistics in a totally incorrect way," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It's clear that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on our part will be known the following day. For us to give confidence to the market, it's good to give transparency." \par \par \uc2  Market transparency is one of the topics that Brazil is covering with an International Monetary Fund (IMF) team that is arriving this weekend to review a $41.5 billion rescue package that had been hammered out before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The IMF agreed to provide the credit along with other international financing bodies to prevent Brazil's economy from collapsing completely as long as the government pushed through the reforms necessary to meet fiscal goals. \par \par \uc2  Lopes said Brazil should be able to meet the target on the primary budget, which excludes debt costs, to get a surplus of 2.6 percent of gross domestic product in 1999 and to hold net public sector debt at 42.5 percent of GDP. ((-Rio de Janeiro newsroom +55 21 507 4151)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1t05j1l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
