\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  debt up on stocks rise,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  rumour. \b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 1999\par 17:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Brazilian dollar debt prices rallied on Tuesday, bolstered by a sharp rise in the country's share prices and suspicion among London traders that the Brazilian government may have been supporting the market. \par \par \uc2  The rise in Brazil's benchmark "C" Brady bond flew in the face of a fresh plunge in the real early in the session to within a whisker of two per dollar and also followed a cut in the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt rating by Fitch IBCA. \par \par \uc2  One trader said market speculation that demand for the debt was coming from a buyer on behalf of the Brazilian government helped drive prices higher. \par \par \uc2  "I'm sure about it. They took all the bonds from the Street," said one Brady bond trader at a European bank. "There were a lot of Brazilian C bonds traded at 49-3/4 and then suddenly everything came back." \par \par \uc2  Others said technical features of the market resulting from local hedging practises squeezed prices higher. \par \par \uc2  "There is no upper limit to the futures market in Brazil today so that has given the market some liquidity to be able to hedge," said one Brady trader based at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Our market is a function of the local markets in Brazil, especially with the Bovespa up pretty strong today," he added. \par \par \uc2  At 1500 GMT, Brazilian "C" bonds were up 2-1/8 at 51-7/8 bid compared with earlier levels below the critical 50 level. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Sao Paolo Bovespa share index\tab    was up 5.6 percent at 7.590 and the newly floating real snapped back to 1.89 to the dollar compared with earlier levels of 1.96 to the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market analysts said comments from Brazil's interim \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Francisco Lopes to a Senate panel which is considering his nomination for bank president also helped prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Lopes said Brazil had a solid position with $36 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and expected to end the year with reserves of around $50 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The sharp rise in Brazilian debt also helped boost other Latin debt prices. Argentine FRBs\tab \tab  were 1-1/4 better at 77 bid and the Mexican UMS global bonds due 2026 were 3/4 firmer at 103 bid.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Diane Craft, London Newsroom +44 171 542 5110, fax 583 7239, uk.emeringmarkets.news@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1q05umq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
