\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Brazil real plunges ahead of IMF meeting. \b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 1999\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In Sao Paulo story headlined "Brazil real plunges ahead of \par \par \uc2  IMF meeting" please read in 17th paragraph French Finance \par \par \uc2  Minister  Dominique Strauss-Kahn ... instead of ....French \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Dominique Strauss-Kahn (correcting title) \par \par \uc2  By John Miller \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Brazil's fragile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged to a new low on Tuesday as officials prepared for talks with the International Monetary Fund amid growing global concern over Latin America's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  IMF technical experts were scheduled to arrive in Brasilia on Tuesday to pave the way for talks on the future of the $41.5 billion IMF-sponsored international rescue package that Brazil received last year. \par \par \uc2  The money was intended to support Brazil's relentless, four-year drive to back the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which officials were forced to float on Jan. 15 as panicked investors withdrew dollars from the country. \par \par \uc2  Investors got another jolt on Tuesday when London-based Fitch IBCA became the latest international rating agency to cut Brazil's credit ratings, an indication the world's eighth-largest economy was one step closer to defaulting on its massive debt. \par \par \uc2  The real opened on Tuesday at 1.83 to the dollar but quickly plunged to about 1.94 per dollar, approaching the psychological barrier of 2 reais per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The devaluation just keeps going and people don't know when it's going to stop. It's stressing out all of the markets," said Roque Sut Ribeiro, a fund manager at Banco Marka in Rio de Janeiro. \par \par \uc2  Still, stocks on Brazil's key Bovespa index were up 2.8 percent at the open on Tuesday as investors hunted for bargains. \par \par \uc2  Since allowing the real to float earlier this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  only once to support it, preferring to let markets choose a new value for the once-strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The real's fall represents a 37.5 percent devaluation since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first relaxed its rigid exchange policy on Jan. 13. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plummet was accompanied by more worrying signals from abroad about Brazil's ability to dig itself out of its worst financial crisis in years. \par \par \uc2  "The longer the exchange rate remains under pressure, the more likely it is that inflation rises, the higher interest rates will be," Fitch IBCA's Richard Fox told Reuters Television. "It just makes it all more difficult." \par \par \uc2  Duff & Phelps Credit Rating Service said on Monday that it saw a 1-in-3 risk that Brazil would default on its 300 billion reais local debt because of huge debt servicing costs related to years of sky-high interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Economists expected little news from this week's IMF visit, at which technical experts will swap facts ahead of talks involving higher-level officials, who will assess Brazil's adherence to an IMF-sponsored programme of economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Instead, Brazilian officials hope markets will take some relief in the passage of a crucial pension reform bill, expected to go before the Senate on Tuesday evening. The bill, which is expected to be passed, would raise contributions paid by federal civil servants and make public-sector workers pay for their pensions for the first time. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempted to soothe investors by merging the two rates at which dollars are exchanged with the real. This sent a signal it did not intend to prevent investors from pulling cash out of the country - a measure Malaysia resorted to late last year during its financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's efforts to contain the crisis have so far received nods of encouragement from world powers. \par \par \uc2  French Finance Minister Dominique Strauss-Kahn said on Tuesday that Brazil should survive its current economic crisis because it was following a proper course of adjustment. \par \par \uc2  But even U.S. officials were bracing for the effects of a prolonged recession in Latin America's leading economy. \par \par \uc2  "We're sort of in a little race against time here. We obviously would like to see Japan and Asia lift up while other countries in the world go through adjustments," said Deputy U.S. Trade Representative Richard Fisher on a visit to Japan. \par \par \uc2  "This makes us even more eager for Japan to resume economic growth and to begin to sort of carry the load in Asia as the economy in Brazil and perhaps some of their neighbours slows down." \par \par \uc2  In Brazil, newspapers spoke of growing restlessness over the government's ability to handle the crisis. \par \par \uc2  An editorial in the leading Sao Paulo newspaper Folha de S.Paulo praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move on Monday for increasing market transparency, but said that in the current siege mentality, it was doubtful it would boost confidence. \par \par \uc2  "The scene at the moment is like the sinking of the Titanic. While the ship was going down, its musicians insisted on continuing to play pleasing sonnets. An edifying option, but with no practical results," the editorial said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1q05ubk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
