\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's real at 1.78/dlr in light midday trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 1999\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Jan 25 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered at 1.78 reais to the dollar when traders broke for lunch on Monday after wide swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate due to very light trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The real was down 3.4 percent from Friday's close of 1.72 per dollar and heading for an all-time low. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closes at the same level, the real's devaluation since Jan. 13 will reach 32 percent. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that because the majority of Sao Paulo banks that carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades are closed for a holiday to celebrate the city's founding, trading was very light and volatile. \par \par \uc2  "Today's price is a reflection of isolated \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and doesn't really show what the tendency will be when trading begins tomorrow," a dealer at a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's benchmark Selic interbank interest rate was unchanged from Friday at 32.5 percent annualized. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the state-owned Banco do Brasil selling dollars on Friday, traders said, to prevent a deeper devaluation. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday because the amount of money involved in the trades is minimal. \par \par \uc2  ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511 248 5408)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1p060ug\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw1