\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil sound, don't overplay  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  moves-Ciampi. \b0\par\par\uc2 180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1999\par 13:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ROME, Jan 22 (Reuters) - Italian Treasury Minister Carlo Azeglio Ciampi said on Friday that Brazil's economy and political system were sound and that its exchange rate fluctuations should not be overplayed. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil is economically and politically solid enough to overcome the current crisis and I do not see specific risks," Ciampi told reporters after a cabinet meeting. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil's economy is solid and we must not attach excessive importance to the exchange rate fluctuations," he said. "Crises are not solved in days." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday for the first time since the real was floated a week ago, selling dollars to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the real tumbled to an all-time low of 1.72 per dollar on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But after firming on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said the embattled Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later swung back to 1.72 per dollar. ((Rome newsroom +39-06-8540049, fax +39-06-8540568 rome. newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1m06537\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
