\par\par \b \uc2  Midday CSCE coffee mixed in seesaw trade, off lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1999\par 18:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 22 (Reuters) - CSCE arabica coffee futures bounced back from an earlier sell-off and traded mixed in brisk, volatile dealings at midsession, lifted by local and speculative shortcovering, market sources said. \par \par \uc2  Speculative and origin selling amid weak technicals and continued worries about the financial crisis in Brazil pushed the benchmark March contract to a low of 104.25 cents per lb, its lowest level since Oct 30, they said. \par \par \uc2  But at 1226 EST, benchmark March coffee stood at 105.60 cents, off 0.40 cent, nearer to the high of 106.75 cents. \par \par \uc2  Second-month May was 0.60 cent weaker, at 107.70 cents, while the rest of the board was down 1.25 to up 0.25 cent. \par \par \uc2  "The market turned around on a dime," said one floor trader. "There was spec and local shortcovering and there was no more selling. I think the market is oversold a bit." \par \par \uc2  Brazil economic problems have helped keep coffee prices under pressure as of late. The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, has depreciated 30 percent since Jan 13, when the government devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before allowing it to float freely two days later. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time since it free-floated the real, defending the once long-stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from engaging in a freefall. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets via state-owned Banco do Brasil shortly after the open, when the real plunged to as low as 1.77 per dollar. The real quickly firmed to 1.65 following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but swung back to its opening level of 1.74 before finishing the morning session at 1.69 reais. \par \par \uc2  Traders reckon the weak real is likely to prompt growers from Brazil to increase their exports of dollar-denominated coffee to realise greater profits. \par \par \uc2  "The Brazil situation sparked this," said another trader, adding, "I don't think Brazil has sold that much, not yet, because the real keeps on devaluating and producers are trying to see where it will stop and then they'll stop selling. But speculators are anticipating the selling." \par \par \uc2  Technically, traders pegged March support at 103.50-103.00 cents, with key psychological support at 100.00 cents. Resistance was seen at 107.00 cents. \par \par \uc2  Volume as of 1200 EST reached an estimated 11,088 lots. \par \par \uc2  ((-Patricia Avidan, New York Commodity Desk, 212-859-1640, patricia.avidan@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1m061id\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
