\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Brazil's Cenbank defends real from freefall. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Noriko Yamaguchi \par \uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1999\par 18:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Jan 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time since it free-floated the real, defending the once long-stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from engaging in a freefall. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets via state-owned Banco do Brasil shortly after the open, when the real plunged to as low as 1.77 per dollar - posting a depreciation of 31.5 percent since Jan. 13, when the goverment devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before allowing it to float freely two days later. \par \par \uc2  The real quickly firmed to 1.65 following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but swung back to its opening level of 1.74 before finishing the morning session at 1.69 reais. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sending a message to the market, although not that powerful at this point, that it will not leave the real run out of control," said one trader at an international bank in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  Markets predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent more than $100 million in trying to defend the real by 1300 local/1500 gmt. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the market was testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point on what level it would come in to rescue the real and how it would step in, after the Bank said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "occasionally and in a limited fashion" at the time of the flotation last Friday. \par \par \uc2  "For a while 1.6 reais to the dollar seemed an appropriate rate. But consensus has shifted away from that due to dollar liquidity problems. Now, it's also a confidence problem. A lot of people out there are scared," said Constantin Jancso, an analyst at MCM Consultores. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept silent about traders' reports today that it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But according to traders, the Bank used Banco do Brasil on Friday, like it has always done when it used to supply dollars to markets and maintain the real trading within a set band. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to study how it does this from now, in a floating market, because the way it is now, everybody short of dollars will run to Banco do Brasil," said one economist who requested anonymity. \par \par \uc2  One way of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to sell dollars randomly through a number of banks, without letting the market know, or the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come in directly and sell dollars through auctions. \par \par \uc2  While the real appeared to settle at around 1.70 reais after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, it was too early to determine this was the level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hoped to maintain the real, some \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders added. \par \par \uc2  Traders predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in Friday more in an effort to supply dollars rather than set any level. \par \par \uc2  Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came one day after the real had tumbled 7.6 percent to finish at an all-time low of 1.72 reais, due to a depletion of dollars in the market amid a tidal wave of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows. \par \par \uc2  Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets lost a net $411 million on Thursday, up from a daily $300 million outflows that had been fleeing since last Friday, and more were expected to escape. \par \par \uc2  About $700 million in private corporate eurobonds were still expected to come due this month, bringing the total amount of overseas debt due to $4.4 billion, including $1.2 billion in interest payments. \par \par \uc2  Another $2.8 billion was seen maturing in February. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made it clear that with the new policy it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to iron out market volatility. It's basically anybody's guess how and when that will work out." \par \par \uc2  "The problem is there are substantial amounts of debt coming due, combined with limited dollar liquidity. Banks are starting to hold on to their dollars," said MCM's Jancso. "By letting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened a Pandora's box and things are flying out of it." \par \par \uc2  ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511 248 5423, noriko.yamaguchi@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1m05xvh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
