\par\par \b \uc2  =Brazil's Real Float Could Weaken U.S. Bonds, Boost Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Blackstone \par \uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 1999\par 18:36\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Brazil's decision to float its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar probably will lead to further liquidation of safe-haven Treasury bond positions when New York trading resumes Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers, largely sidelined Monday for a U.S. public holiday, are expected to bid the dollar higher when they return to their desks Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Monday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the real will be allowed to float freely against the dollar. That decision came after last Friday's decision by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the real. \par \par \uc2  Both actions convinced a lot of observers that Brazil was taking the rights steps to prevent its problems from getting worse or spreading, in turn making it safe for investors to start buying stocks again. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, U.S. and Latin American stocks rallied Friday while the Treasury's 30-year bond lost a point. \par \par \uc2  Latin American stocks were higher again Monday, and analysts expected that bid to extend to New York shares Tuesday, when U.S. markets reopen. Stock and bond markets in the U.S. were closed Monday for Martin Luther King Day. The dollar should see support for the same reasons. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that (Latin American markets) are up right now bodes well for the dollar," said Robert Fullem, corporate trader at NatWest Global Financial Markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  Around midday in New York, the dollar was up slightly against the yen from where it closed last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The downside for Treasurys could be limited by the appearance of Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, who is scheduled to testify before the House Wednesday. Greenspan's expected to be quizzed extensively about Brazil's impact on the U.S. economy, and investors might want to hold off making substantial portfolio shifts before that event. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't think people want to take large positions either way\par especially with Greenspan speaking Wednesday," said Danyelle Guyatt,\par fixed-income strategist at Deutsche Bank in London.\par -By Brian Blackstone and Marianne Sullivan; 201 938-4156\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  01-18-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1236EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4216 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv1i003ou\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
