\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Floats Real In Bid To Stem Capital Flight, Conserve Reserves \b0\par\par\uc2 1,588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 10:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Yielding to pressures from the international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Brazil on Friday set its embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  free to trade unhindered against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The move to float the real, which should allow Brazil to conserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and reduce crippling interest rates, was viewed favorably by investors. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a respected \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  veteran rescinded the resignation he announced Thursday, helping to allay the fears about the nation's deteriorating fiscal picture that rocked world markets this week. And the country's finance minister will head to Washington for high-level talks about the financial crisis with Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin and other top U.S. and international monetary officials. \par \par \uc2  In allowing the real to float freely, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  revoked the new trading band set for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. That move, which widened the band, resulting in an effective devaluation of about 8%, roiled world markets. Following Friday's announcement, the real fell by another 12% before stabilizing in afternoon dealings. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't say that it was abandoning the band altogether, only that it wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Friday to keep the real within the latest band, whose ceiling was 1.32 reals to the dollar. That was the level the real closed at Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly sold dollars to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank promised to issue a full communique regarding the country's exchange-rate policy on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Investors across the globe focused on the positives in the latest policy shift. By giving up its costly defense of the real, Brazil will have more room to reduce interest rates to boost its recessionary economy. A weaker real also makes the price of Brazilian exports more competitive, which could create jobs and help lead the economy out of recession. That, in turn, would go a long way in fending off any serious downdraft in Latin America as a whole, which is a significant U.S. trading partner. \par \par \uc2  "So the bottom line is that Brazil's economy could actually do better than we anticipated only yesterday," said Sung Won Sohn, chief economist at Wells Fargo in Minneapolis. \par \par \uc2  The move to float the real, which had been widely anticipated, came after senior Clinton administration officials and the International Monetary Fund warned Brazil's president, Fernando Henrique Cardoso, that his government wouldn't likely to be able to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at artificial levels in the face of an exodus of foreign investors, the New York Times reported Friday. They told Cardoso Brazil might waste billions of dollars of its precious foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves attempting to do so, the paper said. \par \par \uc2  The government had also come under pressure to let the real float so it wouldn't have to maintain the high interest rates used to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at stronger valuations. The nation's steep rates, which were effective in stamping out hyperinflation earlier in the decade, have shackled the economy. Brazil's benchmark rate stands at a smothering 29%. \par \par \uc2  Cardoso said Friday that the decision to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float was designed to protect the nation's reserves, adding that the move helped reduce the level of distrust in Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Pedro Malan, speaking to reporters after meeting with Cardoso, said, "After the exchange rate shot through the ceiling (of the trading band), we thought it better to let the market act freely to find its own balance." He addded that investors' reaction Friday was "an expression of confidence" in the decision not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  However, the float raises questions about the $41.5 billion, IMF-led bailout of Brazil announced last fall. Indeed, that likely will be one of the issues on the table when Malan visits Washington this weekend. Malan, who will be accompanied by Francisco Lopes, the new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, said he and Lopes will meet with officials of the IMF, the World Bank, the Inter-American Development Bank and the U.S. Treasury. \par \par \uc2  The rescue package assembled for Brazil was designed to keep investor panic from flooding Latin America. In exchange, Cardoso agreed to slash the enormous federal budget deficit to persuade investors to keep their money in the country. The IMF agreement also envisioned Brazil maintaining its exchange-rate policy. But Cardoso was only able to persuade politicians to accept some of his budgetary proposals, resulting in investor flight and the devaluation of the real on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The latest developments have put the IMF in a tough spot. IMF and Brazilian officials may have to renegotiate the loan-aid terms, since the devaluation will increase the amount the government must pay to service its considerable debts and throw it off its IMF-endorsed deficit targets. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to pull back from further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the real "appears to be a wise move" that will protect the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. An IMF spokesperson said the Brazilian government contacted the fund before its latest move. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether Friday's events will lead to any changes in the aid package, Treasury Secretary Rubin replied: "To the best of my knowledge, no." \par \par \uc2  But if Cardoso can't follow through on his fiscal promises, the IMF and its backers at the Treasury might have to decide whether to cut off the rescue effort. And there is recent precedent for the global lending agency doing just that. Since Russia effectively devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and declared a moratorium on some domestic debt in August, the IMF has refused to provide new funding to Moscow, for fear of throwing good money after bad. The Russian moves sparked the run on Brazil last September. \par \par \uc2  The markets, which were buoyed across Europe and the Americas following Brazil's move to float the real, have now turned their attention to what permanent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system Brazil will adopt and where the real will finally settle. Most economists rule out the possibility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  even attempting to return to the sort of rigid system that Brazil had clung to for the past four years. Rather, they say the bank will either stick to the free float or adopt an extremely wide band for the real. \par \par \uc2  Allowing the real to float to weaker levels will increase the price Brazilians pay for essential imported goods and could reignite the inflation the Cardoso government stamped out with its previous monetary policies. But the deep recession and high interest rates are seen mitigating the inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  At the close Friday, the real was trading at about 1.43 to the dollar, down 8.3% from Thursday's close of 1.32 to the dollar. Earlier, the real had slipped 12%, trading as low as 1.50 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The main Bovespa stock index, which had plunged 25% since the start of the year through Thursday, rebounded 33% Friday, on hopes that the worst may behind Brazil - namely, that the weaker real will eventually allow the government to maintain reserves, trim rates and revitalize the economy. \par \par \uc2  But the country continues to face daunting challenges, especially as it tries to reach its goal of shaving $28 billion off its deficit in 1999. And fears likely will continue that the turmoil in the world's eighth-largest economy could spread across Latin America and lead to a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in other emerging-market economies. \par \par \uc2  The crisis deepened Thursday as financial markets took another beating and the government struggled to support the real in the wake of Wednesday's devaluation. One factor undercutting confidence was the announced resignation of Claudio Mauch, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's director responsible for supervising the country's banking system. However, on Friday Mauch rescinded his resignation following a request from Malan and Lopes. \par \par \uc2  Mauch's resignation announcement was poorly timed because it closely followed Wednesday's surprise resignation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Gustavo Franco. Although Mauch stressed that his planned departure was for personal reasons, the news raised worries that Brazil's top monetary officials might be abandoning a sinking ship. \par \par \uc2  Another main concern among investors has been the capital fleeing Brazil. A reported $1.79 billion flowed out of the country Thursday, up from the $1.09 billion in outflows Wednesday. Amid the renewed optimism Friday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported just $160 million in net outflows from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Significantly, there was no capital drawn from the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, said spokeswoman Silvia Faria. \par \par \uc2  "For the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the most favorable results Friday were that - despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's absence - the market didn't appear to be left without liquidity ... and that the exchange rate didn't skyrocket," she said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil reportedly has about $44 billion in dollar reserves, a still-sizable amount, but down by almost half of what it held before the Russian crisis erupted in August. \par \par \uc2  The wave of capital flight intensified over the past week following the announcement by Minas Gerais - Brazil's third-wealthiest state - of a 90-day moratorium on debt payments to the federal government. The state's move sparked fears that the central government wouldn't have the revenue to meet its deficit-cutting goals. \par \par \uc2  Cardoso said Friday that "irresponsible" comments on further state debt moratoria "forced Brazilians as well as foreigners to withdraw their funds from Brazil," leading to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy shift Friday. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv1f01p20\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
