\par\par \b \uc2  U.K., European Banks Rise As Brazil Allows  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Float \b0\par\par\uc2  By Catherine Taylor \par \uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 16:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (Dow Jones)--European banks stocks are up Friday, following Brazil's decision to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float. \par \par \uc2  Despite significant exposure to Brazil, bank shares were higher on the view that floating the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had removed artificial supports. European bank stocks also got hammered earlier in the week, when Brazil effectively devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 1515 GMT, The Dow Jones Stoxx banking index was up 1.1%, or 3.22 points, at 301.85 \par \par \uc2  The U.K. retail bank sector was down 0.37%. The index lost 7.8%, since the start of the week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said investors had overestimated the risk from Brazil earlier this week. Latin American markets account for 5% or less of most European banks' earnings. \par \par \uc2  Further, many banks have been cutting their exposure. U.K. banks, for example, have scaled back their already small exposure to riskier trading activities in emerging markets such as derivatives. And Latin America is not nearly as significant a component of U.K. banks' emerging market portfolios as Asia. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday it wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, allowing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, to float freely. That move follows an effective 8% devaluation of the real earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month the country's third-wealthiest state announced a 90-day moratorium on debt payments to the federal government. \par \par \uc2  Among continental European banks, which have greater exposure than U.K. banks, the Spanish and Germany banks have the most to lose from a collapse in Brazil. \par \par \uc2  But although many market-watchers, like Paribas European banking analyst Romain Burnand, are reserving judgment on whether the caution investors are displaying towards the sector in Continental Europe is warranted, they say that European banks in general had a less risky lending portfolio than in other emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  "Latin America is an environment which was always regarded as risky anyway," Burnand said. "This was not the case in Asia, which was regarded as a booming economy." Loans made in Asia were higher risk, he said, whereas those made in Latin America were "much more selected and protected." \par \par \uc2  Another analyst was more forthright, saying he regarded Continental European banking stocks as well as U.K. banking stocks as oversold. "The market for European banks in aggregate is getting better," he said, adding "investors have overreacted to the Brazilian crisis." \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 15-01-99 \par \par \uc2  1536GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv1f00czo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
