\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Argentine shares fly after six day doze. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carlos A. DeJuana \par \uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 21:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Argentine shares roared back to life Friday after Brazil, the country's main trading partner, put an end to days of nail-biting by regional brokers and let its battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float free. \par \par \uc2  "The news is calming, at least," said Mario Zawadzki, a trader with Schweber brokerage. \par \par \uc2  The leading MerVal index of traded shares was over 10 percent higher in afternoon trade in encouraging turnover of 31.7 million pesos. \par \par \uc2  Led by an over 30 percent rise in Brazilian stocks, Argentina's surge followed a 23 percent drop since Jan. 6, when a rebellious Brazilian state sent markets reeling by postponing debt payments to the central government. \par \par \uc2  By Wednesday, speculative pressure on the real forced the Brazilian government to widen a thin band in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded, devaluing it by about 8 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the outflows failed to cease and \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Friday morning, effectively allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float. The real fell another 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  Argentine banking stocks, hit hard the days before, skyrocketed on expectations that the fall would mean lower interest rates and higher prices for their bond holdings. \par \par \uc2  Banco Frances de la Plata SA  was up nearly 16 percent after slipping almost 34 percent in the prior six days of trade. \par \par \uc2  Telecommunications stocks were also shooting higher, with Telecom Argentina  up 17.7 percent and Telefonica Argentina SA  15.7 percent stronger. \par \par \uc2  "If the scenario in Brazil turns out positive, the risk for Argentina will be limited a lack of economic activity, but we won't have solvency problems or troubles with our financial system," said Paula Premrou, head analyst at Lopez Leon brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Argentina, which sends about a third of its exports to Brazil, has already cut its growth expectations for 1999 on an expected slowdown in economic activity. The government's most recent forecast is a 3.0 percent rise in gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Premrou said many of the companies with strong trade links to Brazil had already seen their share prices dive last year. \par \par \uc2  "Steel and auto stocks have already been tremendously punished," she said. \par \par \uc2  Automaker Sevel  has slipped from last year's high of 1.12 pesos to 0.31 pesos Thursday, an over 70 percent slide. \par \par \uc2  ((Buenos Aires Newsroom, 541 510-2505, buenosaires.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1f06gdp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
