\par\par \b \uc2  IMF says Brazil devaluation could stem dollar flight. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Janet Guttsman \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 23:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Jan 15 (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund welcomed Brazil's decision to stop defending its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, changing tack to describe it as a wise move which could stem the flood of dollars from the country. \par \par \uc2  A spokesman said the IMF, brains behind a $41.5 billion international rescue package for Brazil, had been informed in advance about \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's abrupt decision to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the once-proud real. "It appears to be a wise move to stop the loss of reserves," he said. \par \par \uc2  The announcement marked a none-too-subtle change of course for the IMF, which had so far steered clear of endorsing a floating exchange rate for Brazil, the world's eighth largest economy and Latin America's biggest economy by far. \par \par \uc2  IMF officials said when they announced the loan package last November that they expected Brazil to accelerate the pace of devaluation by widening the sloping corridor within which the real was allowed to trade against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But they said Brazil planned to continue its exchange rate regime - a crawling peg with neither the flexibility of a floating rate nor the rigid rules adopted by neighbouring Argentina, where one peso is worth exactly one dollar. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's letter of intent to the IMF, a 35-point document outlining government proposals, said the government, which viewed the real as the cornerstone of its anti-inflation policy, was fully committed to a stable real. \par \par \uc2  "The priority goal is continued low inflation," said the letter, sent to the IMF on Nov. 13. "The current exchange rate regime is an essential means to this end." \par \par \uc2  Economists, who have long complained that the real was overvalued, broadly welcomed Brazil's change of plan, viewing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new exchange rate of around 1.42 to the dollar as more sustainable than the one which went before. \par \par \uc2  "This may be the first emerging market devaluation that actually works, that boosts confidence rather than undermines it," said Arturo Porzecanski, chief economist for the Americas at ING-Barings in New York. \par \par \uc2  "For better or for worse, Brazil now has a more realistic exchange rate that reflects market pressures - but nothing has happened to change underlying market pressures." \par \par \uc2  Markets had punished Brazil after a regional governor's decision to default on some debts highlighted a rift between Brasilia and the provinces and cast doubts over the government's ability to cut spending as prescribed by the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Billions of dollars flooded out of Brazil and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first widened the real's trading band against the dollar and then stopped defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely. \par \par \uc2  The real dropped to 1.58 to the dollar before bouncing back to steady at around 1.41. Brazilian shares, under pressure for days, reversed course abruptly and soared 33 percent. \par \par \uc2  A weaker real means far heavier debt servicing costs for Brazil and changes many of the assumptions behind the rescue package. Brazilian officials will arrive in Washington on Saturday for talks with the IMF and the U.S. Treasury. \par \par \uc2  "This trip is to make amends, to break down the very frosty relationship that had developed in recent weeks," Porzecanski said. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian rescue deal was the fifth multi-billion dollar bailout in 18 months after packages in Asia and Russia. Mexico won its own big bailout in 1995. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said Brazil was not as worrisome as the IMF's previous patients. Banks were better developed and the government was committed to change even if it was finding it hard to persuade Congress to approve painful spending cuts. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil is very different from both Mexico and Asia," said David Hale, of Zurich Kemper Investments. \par \par \uc2  "There will be some shocks and there will be risks, but I just don't think Brazil, because of its historical circumstances, has to have the kind of shocks we saw in Mexico a few years ago and in Asia over the last two years." \par \par \uc2  Most economists did not expect Brazil to need extra financing at this stage, although World Bank President James Wolfensohn said he was ready to consider accelerating loan payments to help Brazil out of its crisis. \par \par \uc2  "I would consider it. I may consider it positively, I may consider it negatively, but I will consider it," Wolfensohn told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The World Bank has promised to contribute $4.5 billion to the Brazilian package. It has approved payments totalling just over $1 billion, but has not yet handed over the money because Brazil's Congress has not yet approved the loan. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1f06gbq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
