\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil devaluation offers opportunity for Argentina. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Axel Bugge \par \uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 20:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Argentine economic growth may suffer from a devaluation in Brazil, but the move by its northern neighbour could offer Buenos Aires an opportunity to show off its stronger fundamentals, economists said on Friday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, leaving its embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, to float. The move came two days after Latin America's largest economy devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by about 8 percent. \par \par \uc2  "This is a chance for Argentina to differentiate itself" from Brazil, said Ricardo Daud, a fund manager at Bansud. \par \par \uc2  Economists have long believed the biggest threat to Argentina's economy is its proximity to Brazil, its biggest trading partner. An economic blow-out in Brazil could have a ripple effect in Argentina, just as Mexico's devaluation did in December 1994. \par \par \uc2  During the "Tequila Crisis," investors hurried for the exit door and savers withdrew about 20 percent of the banking system's deposits on fears Argentina would drop its peso peg to the dollar. Argentina's economy contracted 4.6 percent in 1995. \par \par \uc2  Now, there are worries that Brazil's decision to float its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have the same effect. \par \par \uc2  But with the economy having changed drastically since 1995 - most notably by reforms of the banking sector - the government is betting things will be very different now. \par \par \uc2  Buenos Aires is counting on investors to remember that the country rode out the 1995 storm without any doubt about its maintaining the peso's one-to-one peg to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The 1995 experience helped us a lot," said Gustavo Canonero, head of research for Mercosur at Deutsche Bank Global Markets. "It really demonstrated that there is strong support across all elements of society (for government economic policies)." \par \par \uc2  The biggest change in the economy since 1995 has been a buying spree of Argentine banks by foreign ones, meaning huge multinational financial institutions now account for more than 40 percent of bank deposits, compared with half that in 1995. \par \par \uc2  Banks' cash reserves have been sharply bolstered by strict new minimum liquidity requirements and the government has a $6.1 billion standby liquidity credit facility for the banking system that it can draw on in case of an emergency. Bank deposits are currently at record highs. \par \par \uc2  This means that now, unlike in 1995, savers will leave their money in the banks. "People will not withdraw their deposits," Daud said. \par \par \uc2  Another measure of investor confidence, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, is currently at record highs thanks to government efforts to return to Argentina some $7 billion in reserves held abroad by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A lot of the confidence in Argentina comes from an Extended Fund Facility deal with the International Monetary Fund. This agreement offers $2.8 billion that can be drawn down when needed, and it builds confidence among investors because of the external monitoring of Argentina's fiscal accounts carried out by the IMF, economists said. \par \par \uc2  It includes economic targets, which is one of the reasons the government has worked hard to reduce the budget deficit to about 1 percent of gross domestic product, far from Brazil's 7.45 percent. \par \par \uc2  An IMF spokesman voiced support for Argentina on Friday, saying the IMF "would be willing to help if needed." \par \par \uc2  The big uncertainty, however, is how much growth will be stunted by the higher interest rates resulting from Brazil's devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Those higher rates have already prevented Argentina from raising money in international markets, and lack of credit for Argentina's small firms will cut deep into their pockets. \par \par \uc2  Some economists say there will be a recession in 1999, but the government stuck to its 3 percent growth estimate this week. \par \par \uc2  For interest rates to come down, allowing growth to bounce back up, the government will have to convince international investors that the risks of putting money into Argentina are not as great as they think. \par \par \uc2  To do that, the government has to be able to convince investors of its ability to meet its own financing needs. \par \par \uc2  As a result of careful planning at the end of last year, Buenos Aires already has secured the financing it needs for the first half of 1999 through a credit line from international lenders and bond issues to domestic investors. \par \par \uc2  If it drew on the IMF's $2.8 billion it could nearly fill all third-quarter financing needs as well. \par \par \uc2  "I hope we can differentiate (between the Argentine and Brazilian economies)," Canonero said. "That would really be the beginning of a new era for Argentina." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1f06g94\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
