\par\par \b \uc2  Latin America relieved Brazil bites bullet. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Axel Bugge \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 20:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Jan 15 (Reuters) - After the initial shock of a Brazilian devaluation earlier in the week, Latin Americans praised Brazil's decision on Friday to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float and not waste billions of dollars trying to prop it up. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, allowing its embattled real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sink by 12 percent. The move came two days after the world's eighth-largest economy devalued the real by about 8 percent. \par \par \uc2  While tempered by the knowledge that letting the real float would likely drag Brazil further into recession, there was an overwhelming sense of relief by Latin American officials and economists that Brazil had finally thrown in the towel. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that one of the biggest countries in the world, in this case Brazil ..., has taken this decision, represents for all of us a very important and a very positive piece of news," said Mexico's Finance Minister Jose Angel Gurria, who predicted interest rates would fall now that the uncertainty was removed. \par \par \uc2  Ever since Russia's devaluation and debt default in August, Latin Americans had feared the worst would also come to pass with their region's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  When Brazil devalued by about 8 percent on Wednesday there were ominous reminders of Mexico's December 1994 devaluation when it also attempted a managed devaluation only to be followed by a free float a few days later. \par \par \uc2  "Just let it happen," many said, as foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves bled at about $1 billion a day this week. \par \par \uc2  "There was no other alternative," said Ricardo Daud, a fund manager at Bansud in Buenos Aires. "Continuing to kick the ball forward made no sense." \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Brazilian government can now focus its efforts on getting its fiscal situation in order and meeting the targets it set itself last year in a massive expenditure-cutting programme. That programme formed the basis of a $41.5 billion International Monetary Fund-led rescue package Brazil agreed to at the end of last year. \par \par \uc2  "If Brazil passes its austerity measures, and interest rates fall, things are going to get better for the region," said Pablo Secada, an analyst at Santander in Peru. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's Economy Minister Roque Fernandez said he did not think the crisis would last for long because Brazil is pushing ahead with its measures. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the crisis will be long-lived because many of the measures Brazil needs have already been approved, maybe not as fast as markets wanted but they have been passed," Fernandez said. \par \par \uc2  However, as markets headed skyward after Brazil's announcement, some remembered why a Brazilian devaluation was so feared in the first place - because it could unleash an Asian-style crisis of devaluing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominoes. \par \par \uc2  In Colombia, which itself devalued the trading bands of its peso last year, economist Javier Fernandez Riva said the Brazilian move was a "wake-up call." \par \par \uc2  He said the authorities would now likely have to hike interest rates yet further - damaging the slowing economy - or follow Brazil and float. \par \par \uc2  The Colombian peso fell to a historic low as Finance Minister Juan Camilo Restrapo called the Brazil news "disconcerting." \par \par \uc2  But he vowed "there will be no new devaluations in response to those that may occur in neighbouring countries." \par \par \uc2  Venezuela, which faced constant rumours of a devaluation last year, was still benefiting from the honeymoon markets have granted President-elect Hugo Chavez. \par \par \uc2  "The repercussions have not been felt so much here because we have our own fiscal and political problems," said Eduardo Franco of Caracas' Profimerca brokerage. \par \par \uc2  In Ecuador, which also devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Luis Jacome said his country would not follow Brazil's path despite strong pressures on the sucre this week. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to defend the sucre; there is no reason to think about devaluation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is going to calm down," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1f06g5h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
