\par\par \b \uc2  LatAm markets roar after Brazil floats  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carlos A. DeJuana \par \uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 19:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Latin American stocks and bonds shot higher Friday after Brazil's decision to float its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased concerns that the region's largest economy would unravel. \par \par \uc2  Some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also stronger, although most had not fully recovered from an initial dive after Brazil's real quickly depreciated by about 12 percent after the government early Friday abandoned its attempt to keep it within a trading band. \par \par \uc2  By mid-afternoon, Brazil's Bovespa index had surged over 28 percent on news the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the real to trade freely on the market. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's MerVal stock index was over 7.7 percent higher and Mexico's IPC was up more than 6.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  "They're buying for the medium and long term, because prices are rock bottom now," a Mexican desk trader said, echoing regional enthusiasm for severely-punished equities in the region. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shares in Chile were four percent higher, and Venezuela's IBC      index was up 1.5 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Latin American stocks had been sorely battered in the latest round of Brazil's financial crisis, triggered by the Jan. 6 debt moratorium declaration by Minas Gerais state. The rebellious state said it would halt payments for 90 days on $13.4 billion owed to central government.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Spooked investors fled Latin American markets in droves and Brazil haemorrhaged several billion dollars to keep the real stable - a backbone of a five-year-old economic policy to crush inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday's slide in the long-steady real came on top of an 8 percent devaluation Wednesday when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  widened a narrow \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the move failed to inspire investor confidence and $2.8 billion stampeded out of the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by Thursday. The Sao Paulo stock exchange's blue-chip Bovespa index also fell by a cumulative 15 percent on Wednesday and Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday morning \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, effectively allowing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to float.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders seemed relieved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This (the float) was the worst thing everyone was expecting and now it's over," an Argentine trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bond prices, also pummeled recently, surged at the news.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brazilian's "C" bonds - considered the benchmark of Latin America's internationally traded bonds - rose 6.125 points Friday to a bid price of 55.125, Argentina's "FRBS" had gained 5.375 points to 78.625 and Mexico's "PAR" bonds were 2.375 points higher at 73.625.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , initially jolted by the real's dive early Friday, were mixed by midday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mexico's peso was stronger at 10.3510/10.3800 after sliding to 10.73/10.83 in pre-opening bids.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chile's peso had also calmed after the initial shock, trading at 478 per dollar back from 485 at its open. It has lost less than one percent of its value since Tuesday, the day before Brazil's first devaluation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1f06g3y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
