\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  says banking system solid. \b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 21:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Argentina's banking system is virtually bullet proof to the crisis caused by Brazil's devaluation and the Argentine economy is well positioned to cope with Brazil's devaluation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Today the banking system could lose 40 percent of deposits without credit being affected," said an internal \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  memo which Reuters obtained Friday from a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  source. \par \par \uc2  During the worst moment of the 1995 Tequila crisis following Mexico's peso devaluation, some 18 percent of the banking system's deposits were withdrawn, the memo said. \par \par \uc2  Commenting on expectations in the government on the impact on Argentina's economy of Brazil's devaluation, the memo listed a number of points which should reduce the damaging impact. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, leaving its embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, to float. \par \par \uc2  "As an element in its favour, the (Economy Ministry) points out that Argentina's economy has already proven itself by overcoming the (1995) Tequila effect, the Asian crisis and more recently, Russia's default as well," the memo said. \par \par \uc2  The memo said the "Economy Ministry leadership" considers Brazil's devaluation will have an impact on economic activity "which will be most felt in certain determined sectors, but that in no way is the country's macro-economic balance or its fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg at risk." \par \par \uc2  Many economists have said Argentina's economy could be hit hard because a third of its exports go to Brazil but the memo pointed out that exports represent just 8 percent of Argentina's gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Anyway, most of the the exports are commodity products which could easily be sent to other markets, it said. \par \par \uc2  "In sum, the global impact of Brazil's devaluation will be slight in commercial terms and shouldn't affect more than 1 percent of Argentina's GDP," the memo said. \par \par \uc2  However, the memo warned of a "psychological phenomenon, whereby foreign investors tend to associate Argentina with what happens in Brazil." That impact should make it more difficult to raise foreign financing. \par \par \uc2  But near the end it said: "Without downplaying the importance of Brazil's crisis, the Economy Ministry leadership is confident a rebound in the economy can be relatively quick. They pointed out that recent history showed that during the Tequila the economy took a year to recover and after the Russian default it took just three months." \par \par \uc2  Lastly, it pointed to politics, saying that while in "Brazil there is a power dispute which creates uncertainty, in Argentina there is a president who does not hesitate to veto laws which challenge his economic model." ((Buenos Aires Newsroom, +541 318-0668, buenosaires.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1f06frv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
