\par\par \b \uc2  LatAm stocks hard hit by shock Brazil devaluation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Martin Roberts \par \uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 1999\par 06:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Jan 13 (Reuters) - Latin American stocks fell hard in step with equities worldwide on Wednesday as investors' worst fears were confirmed by a devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of regional giant Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Stock markets from Mexico to Argentina all staggered under fallout from Brazil, although by the close they had partly recovered from early panic selling caused by Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Gustavo Franco's sudden resignation and the immediate widening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band used to control \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I'm sitting here crying," said trader Oswaldo Vasquez at Gysin brokerage in Buenos Aires, echoing his counterparts from around the region. "Spirits are pretty low." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Argentina was the worst performer in Latin America after next-door neighbour Brazil pummeled the MerVal index\tab    and pushed it down 10.2 percent to 356.16 points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts had expected a Brazilian devaluation at some stage due to hefty capital flight, which totalled an estimated $1.2 billion on Tuesday alone, but were surprised when they woke up to Wednesday's move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I would say the way they did it was worse than anybody expected," said Jorge Mariscal, Latin America investment strategist at Goldman Sachs in New York. "They've done it in a very disorganized, uncoordinated fashion, with no support from the International Monetary Fund, or the (U.S.) Treasury."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brazilian shares plummeted by more than 10 percent and activated circuit breakers after 13 minutes of trade on expectations that a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would send interest rates soaring and shift the economy into a deep recession.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mariscal estimated that Brazil's economy would shrink some 4 percent in 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark Bovespa\tab    index of stocks traded in Brazil's financial capital Sao Paulo ended down 5.05 percent at 5617 points, off a session low of 5278.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But analysts said Wednesday's rebounds could be short lived while doubts remain about whether the real will stay at its new level and how Brazil plans to pay off its sizable internal debt, which nervous investors have been eyeing for months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think it (the partial market recoeso \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Mexican shares have lost 22 percent this year in dollar terms.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Across the Andes in Chile, stock traders saw their benchmark IPSA   index sag 4.97 percent to 89.72 points as jitters spilled over from Brazil.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Venezuela's IBC index\tab   meanwhile slipped 3.0 percent to 4217.78 points in step with the rest of Latin America.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Mexico City newsroom +525 728-7907, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1e06mms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
