\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Cenbank sells dollars in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  mkts-traders. \b0\par\par\uc2 329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 1998\par 14:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Dec 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Monday, selling dollars to offset an outflow of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank was selling dollars through federal bank Banco do Brasil to prevent the real from weakening amid a wave of dollar outflows, they said. \par \par \uc2  The real was quoted at 1.2065 per U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1120 local/1320 gmt, unchanged from Friday's close, but close enough to the current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  mini-band's limit to trigger an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, when the real nudged close to the mini-band limits, which are now set at 1.2070 and 1.1940 reais against the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said dollar outflows have stepped up again this month due to maturing foreign bonds and loans. \par \par \uc2  A net $317 million exited \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday after a net $311 million flew out on Thursday - both days posting substantially higher outflows when compared to a daily net outflow of about $100 million in November. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have already lost a net $2.639 billion so far this month, after a net $1.91 billion exited in November. \par \par \uc2  Dollar flight from Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been closely monitored by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the world's rich nations which have promised Brazil an anti-crisis loan package of $41.5 billion in November. \par \par \uc2  The IMF had put together the massive credit, worried about Brazil's excessive capital flight and its impact on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - the country's main weapon against speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attacks. \par \par \uc2  A senior official at Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters on Friday that the country's reserves now stood at about $49 billion according to the international liquidity concept, which includes accounts receivable. \par \par \uc2  ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511 248 5423, sao.paulo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926ducl01z3c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
