\par\par \b \uc2  Brazilian stocks tumble on Greenspan speech. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shasta Darlington \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 20:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Brazilian equities markets tumbled Thursday, joining Wall Street and Asian bourses after hopes for coordinated rate cuts in the U.S. and other industrialised nations were dashed, market players said. \par \par \uc2  "Markets had been operating on the assumption of a rates cut, and now they're adjusting for the fact that there might not be any," said a fund manager at Banco Patrimonio. \par \par \uc2  Sao Paulo's key Bovespa index crumbled in the first hour of trading, plunging more than 10 percent and setting off the circuit-breaker to halt trade. Later in the session, it reduced its losses to around 7 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Bovespa tumbled along with bourses in Asia, Europe and the United States after Chairman of the U.S. Federal Reserve Alan Greenspan quashed hopes Wednesday that a number of industrialised countries were in talks to lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Greenspan's speech yesterday disappointed the market, which was pressing for an immediate, instant solution (to the global financial turbulence)," said one local trader. \par \par \uc2  Investors were also cautious as they awaited firmer news from international agencies on a support programme reportedly being coordinated by the International Monetary Fund and aimed at shielding Latin America from the global financial turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "There is a continuous dialogue which has been intensified in the last few weeks," a monetary source in Washington said on Thursday of contact between Brazil and the IMF. \par \par \uc2  "It's talks on talks," the source said, adding that Brazil has not asked the IMF for cash. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening as more dollars continued to leave the country, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Two consecutive rate hikes in Brazil and expectations of an international support plan slowed the outflow of dollars on Tuesday and Wednesday, but no new details have surfaced since, and dollars continue to leave the country. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect another $400 million in dollars to wash out of Brazil on Thursday. While low compared to average daily outflows of $1.5 billion last week, the departure of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not stopped. \par \par \uc2  And without more concrete news, many market players worry that the government's recent efforts to fend off a crisis may not be sufficient. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday night, Brazil came up with more spending cuts and belt-tightening measures in a bid to narrow a gaping budget deficit that will be exacerbated by interest rates now jacked up to 50 percent. \par \par \uc2  The government announced five proposals which could reduce federal government spending by as much as 2.1 billion reais ($1.8 billion) between now and the end of the year, ensuring Brazil meets its target of a 5 billion real ($4.23 billion) surplus in its primary account in 1998. \par \par \uc2  That target, included in a decree, was announced last week along with 4 billion reais ($3.38 billion) of federal government spending cuts as Brazilian markets took a beating amid fallout from Russia's devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But the primary account does not include debt costs. The nominal deficit, which does, is nearing a precarious 8 percent of GDP, frightening already skittish investors. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also plans to sell its brand new NBC-F paper for the first time Thursday. The newly-created notes are pegged to the floating exchange rate and are aimed at stemming outflows. \par \par \uc2  Still, many traders said words of support from U.S. leaders were enough to ensure that Brazilian markets will not be headed back for the lows it hit last week. \par \par \uc2  "The worst has passed," Patrimonio's fund manager said. "The United States recognised that Brazil is more important than Russia, more important than Asia to its economy and that's a sign that there is support." \par \par \uc2  ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511 248 5408, shasta.darlington@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9h01bug\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
