\par\par \b \uc2  LatAm bourses battered despite economic measures. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carlos A. DeJuana \par \uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par 00:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Latin American financial markets were hammered on Thursday by investors blind to government efforts to bolster their economies against the financial earthquake rolling across the continents. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian Bovespa index led the dive slipping 6.13 percent in the first 30 minutes of trade and ending 15.8 percent lower, its largest one-day drop in 11 years. \par \par \uc2  The plunge caught other regional markets in its tailwind, dragging bourses down from Mexico City to Buenos Aires. \par \par \uc2  "The order is get out of the market, regardless of price," said Fernando Romero Caranza, a Latin American analyst with ABN Amro in New York. "We're stuck in a perverse cycle of self-fulfilling prophesies. The market wants blood and it is getting it." \par \par \uc2  The Argentine exchange posted the second-harshest fall in the region, finishing down 13.32 percent, and Mexican shares tumbled 9.82 percent. \par \par \uc2  Chile closed down 7.38 percent, its largest one-day percentage drop since Oct. 6, 1998, and Venezuelan shares ended at a 30-month low, off 4.48 percent for the day. \par \par \uc2  Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds also reeled from the turmoil. Mexico's peso closed at a record low of 10.52/10.55 to the U.S. dollar even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times in efforts to shore it up. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark "C" bonds finished down 3.625 at 52.125/52.5. Argentina's FRB bonds closed down 5.25 at 64.5/65.625. \par \par \uc2  Investors fear that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation in Brazil, Latin America's biggest economy, could ripple across Latin America and devastate economies recovering from the 1994 "Tequila crisis" triggered by a devaluation in Mexico. \par \par \uc2  Those concerns pummeled the Brazilian bourse and led to almost $2 billion in net outflows from its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Thursday, Brazilian traders said. \par \par \uc2  That is in addition to about $8.77 billion lost in in the first nine days of September and $12 billion lost in August. \par \par \uc2  "The market doesn't know how long the government will be able to sustain this situation," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President Bill Clinton's problems only worsened the already fragile situation by adding the prospect of impeachment to the world's list of worries, said traders. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones index closed down 3.17 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Clinton is basically affecting the market in the United States, and by affecting the United States, it is affecting us," said Argentine trader Julian Cohen. \par \par \uc2  Investors appear unconvinced by Latin American governments that have moved to shield their economies from the problems that devastated Asia and Russia. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, Brazil announced much-anticipated measures aimed at trimming the country's budget deficit, currently at a gaping 7 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico, Latin America's second largest economy, President Ernesto Zedillo promised to do all he can to end the recurring six-year cycle of economic breakdowns and warned Mexicans of more tough times due to the harsh economic environment in his State of the Union speech last week. \par \par \uc2  Zedillo also sent Congress a series of financial reforms designed to improve the country's banking system. \par \par \uc2  Argentine officials have already cut $2 billion from this year's planned spending and the government passed labour and tax reforms aimed at reducing the financial burden on employers. \par \par \uc2  President Carlos Menem also said he had frozen public spending for next year at 1998 levels. \par \par \uc2  Other countries have also enacted protective measures. \par \par \uc2  Venezuela announced last Friday fresh spending cuts of $540 million, combined with the sale of government assets and a fresh dividend from state oil company PDVSA, which together would total $2 billion. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the reforms have failed to help, mostly because investors are not convinced by Brazil's attempts. \par \par \uc2  "For the size of Brazil's problems, the cuts were insufficient," said Cohen. "They didn't do anything." \par \par \uc2  "Brazil has a lack of credibility, and since it's an important market, that lack of credibility is flowing into other countries in the region," said ABN Amro's Romero Caranza. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00t3i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
