\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - ANALYSIS - Brazil's real seen riding out mkt. \b0\par\par\uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In Sao Paulo item "ANALYSIS - Brazil's real seen riding out mkt storm", please read in eleventh paragraph, "Last Friday, Brazil saw a record one-day net dollar outflow for the year of $2.43 billion," instead of $2.89 billion. Corrects figure. \par \par \uc2  A corrected version follows. \par \par \uc2  By Noriko Yamaguchi \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Brazil's strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should survive the lashing global financial markets have dealt emerging economies recently thanks to high foreign reserves and the promise of ample dollar inflows, economists said Monday. \par \par \uc2  In fact, economists said Brazil was in a stronger position now to support its real against the dollar than it was last October, when speculators tested the government's resolve at the height of the Asian crisis. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil has clearly increased its capability to face a crisis because the level and quality of its foreign reserves have improved," said Odair Abate, chief economist at Lloyds Bank in Brazil. "The country is seen surviving due to the continuous inflow of dollars," Abate said, noting much of the new money is going into long-term investment. \par \par \uc2  World markets keep a close eye on Brazil's real, which is widely seen overvalued by between 10 percent and 30 percent. Brazil is Latin America's largest economy and a sharp devaluation would likely trigger a regional economic slump comparable to the one that followed the 1994 Mexican peso crisis. \par \par \uc2  Russia's troubled economy has sent shockwaves through world markets, and traders on Monday were quick to draw parallels between Brazil and Russia - two giant emerging markets whose governments have failed to tame bloating budget deficits. \par \par \uc2  But Brazilian officials have spent the last two weeks trying to differentiate the two economies, mainly pointing to Brazil's vast $70 billion in reserves versus Russia's $17 billion. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Brazil has a much more stable political outlook, with President Fernando Henrique Cardoso widely expected to be re-elected in October. And Brazil's banking sector has strengthened over the last four years on improved regulation, stiffer competition and a wave of mergers with foreign institutions. \par \par \uc2  "The banking system is in an infinitely better situation now than it was at the height of the Asian crisis," said Roberto Setubal, president of the Federation of Brazilian Banks. \par \par \uc2  Setubal, who is also the president of private retail bank Banco Itau , said Brazilian banks had significantly reduced their leverage and exposure to highly volatile dollar-denominated Brazilian foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  Still, market jitters have not been limited to stocks. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market almost every day for the past two weeks to support the real, which trades within a tightly regulated band against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday, Brazil saw a record one-day net dollar outflow for the year of $2.43 billion. (Corrects figure.) Some of the money fled from local stocks and another chunk went to repay foreign agricultural loans, which had been expected. \par \par \uc2  But economists say there are plenty more dollars coming in to cover the outflows. And they add that the new money is longer term and therefore less likely to flee during market panics. \par \par \uc2  For example, Brazil sold off Latin America's largest telephone operator Telebras in late July, raising $19 billion, of which $5.5 billion has already entered the country. The rest will come in over the next two years. \par \par \uc2  Also, an estimated $3.0 billion is expected to flow into Brazil over the next few weeks to pay for two local banks and a steelmaker, which were snatched up by European firms earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  And several of Brazil's state-owned power utilities remain to be sold, guaranteeing billions more dollars over the next two years. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the inflows will keep reserves at about the $70 billion through the end of the year, a sum equal to 14 months worth of imports. \par \par \uc2  "The reserves underline the government's credibility when it says it will sustain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Cassio Schmitt, senior economist at retail bank Unibanco. \par \par \uc2  ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511-248-5423, noriko.yamaguchi@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8o01dsg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
